attempts.plan:
1. Load the Transport and Crime data into a pandas DataFrame and load the borough‐neighbor adjacency list;  
2. Identify and handle missing values: since Crime_value has one missing entry, drop the borough with missing Crime (and remove it from the adjacency list) so that all remaining boroughs have valid Transport and Crime values;  
3. Construct a spatial weights matrix W (size n×n) from the adjacency list, where W[i,j]=1 if borough i and j are neighbors and 0 otherwise; row‐standardize W so each row sums to 1;  
4. Extract arrays X (Transport values) and Y (Crime values) for the n boroughs; demean X and Y by subtracting their respective means;  
5. Compute the observed Lee’s L statistic:  
   – Numerator: ∑_i ∑_j W[i,j] X[i] Y[j]  
   – Denominator: sqrt[ (∑_i ∑_j W[i,j] X[i] X[j]) × (∑_i ∑_j W[i,j] Y[i] Y[j]) ]  
6. Perform a permutation test with at least 500 random shuffles of Y:  
   a. For each shuffle k, randomly permute Y across boroughs, compute L_k the same way as in step 5;  
   b. Build the null distribution of L_k;  
7. Compute the p‐value as the proportion of |L_k| ≥ |L_observed| (two‐sided) or L_k ≥ L_observed if testing a positive association;  
8. Determine significance: if p<0.05 and L_observed>0 → POSITIVE; if p<0.05 and L_observed<0 → NEGATIVE; otherwise → NOT SIGNIFICANT.
